WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
A pulsating star changes its own structure and

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.460
brightness. Watch its radius and light curve vary

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.180
together. Not every variable works this way: an

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.540
eclipsing binary dims because another star blocks light.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.840
The pulsation shown here is schematic.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.900
Measure from one brightness maximum to the next

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.060
equivalent maximum. That interval is the period. The

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.740
height of the variation is its amplitude, a

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.260
different quantity. Here, the luminous pulsator represents the

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.260
class used in the next distance calibration step.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.040
Its period is measured in days.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.900
Identify the variable’s type and apply its calibrated

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.860
period luminosity relation. Within a given Cepheid calibration,

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.780
longer periods indicate greater luminosity. Then compare intrinsic

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.760
luminosity with received brightness, correcting for extinction. For

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.620
equal luminosity, four times fainter means twice as

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.220
far away.
